WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Africa's volcanoes rise where its plates are stretching apart, or where hot rock wells up from deep inside the Earth.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:11.000
In the west, a line of volcanoes runs from the Gulf of Guinea inland. Nigeria's Biu Plateau is linked to it, and Mount Cameroon is its most active volcano.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:17.000
About 30 million years ago, floods of lava poured out in Ethiopia, most of them within about a million years, and built the Ethiopian highlands.

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.000
In the East African Rift, Kilimanjaro began to grow about two and a half million years ago.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:34.000
In recent decades Mount Cameroon erupted in 1999, 2000 and 2012, and the runny lava of Nyiragongo reached the city of Goma in 2002 and 2021, killing people and forcing many to flee.

6
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.133
The Sahara has a beginning.

7
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:42.267
Wind-blown dune sand about 7 million years old, found in the northern Chad Basin, is the oldest known sign of a desert there.

8
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:46.367
Before that find, most geologists thought the desert was only 2 to 3 million years old.

9
00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:56.000
A later study links the drying to the shrinking of the Tethys Sea, between about 11 and 7 million years ago.

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.800
From about 14,500 years ago, stronger monsoon rains turned the Sahara green, with grassland, lakes and rivers, and Lake Chad grew into a vast Lake Mega-Chad.

11
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.600
The rains weakened between about 6,000 and 5,000 years ago, in steps and at different times in different places.

12
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.400
In the eastern Sahara, archaeology shows people leaving the drying desert for the Nile valley and moving south towards today's Sahel.

13
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.200
South of Lake Chad, farming and herding villages spread from about 1800 BCE.

14
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:28.000
Whether people from the drying desert moved there is not shown by the sources checked so far.
